    "dynamic_instructions": "{\r\n  \"datasetView\": \"**Meet the Data**\\n\\nGoal: Explore your dataset. Pick what your model will predict.\\n\\nA record is all the data about one play. In the table view, each record shows as one row. Scroll right to see every feature.\\n\\n1. Find the record count at the top of the table (\\\"51 rows\\\"). Write it as the total records in **My Data** in your guide.\\n2. Click **Cards** to look through the records one at a time.\\n3. Pick one label for your model to predict (**Play**, **Drive Scored?** or **Team Won the Game?**). Write it in **My Data**.\\n4. Count the records in each category of your label:\\n    - a. Click **Filter**. Choose your label and one category.\\n    - b. Read the count at the top (for example, \\\"20 of 51 rows\\\"). Write it in **My Data**.\\n    - c. Click **Filter**, then **Clear filter**. Repeat for each category.\\n5. Pick two features that could help predict your label (**Down**, **Yards to Go** or **Score**). Do step 4 for each feature.\\n\\nYou are done when **My Data** has your label and two features, each with record counts.\\n\\nThink about: Who could use a model trained on this data?\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Overview**: \\n\\nThis dataset contains information about a variety of different football plays from the NFL.\\n\\n**Data Source**: \\n\\nnflverse play-by-play data. Each record is one real play from the 2023 NFL season\",\r\n  \"results\": \"What can you do on the **Testing** screen?\\n* Interact with your decision tree\\n* Examine the test data\\n* Trace a prediction\\n* Save your decision tree to your device or backpack\"\r\n}",
